{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[{"left":"33","quote":"\\[ 33 = (-33) \\]","right":"-33","span":[779,795]}],"prefix":"Given the polynomial \\( f(x) = 2x^n + a_{n-1}x^{n-1} + \\cdots + a_2x^2 + a_1x + 66 \\) with integer coefficients and distinct integer roots, we can express the polynomial in its factored form as:\n\\[ f(x) = 2(x - r_1)(x - r_2) \\cdots (x - r_n) \\]\nwhere \\( r_1, r_2, \\ldots, r_n \\) are the distinct integer roots. The constant term \\( a_0 \\) is given by:\n\\[ a_0 = 2(-r_1)(-r_2) \\cdots (-r_n) = 66 \\]\nThis implies:\n\\[ 2(r_1 r_2 \\cdots r_n) = 66 \\]\nor\n\\[ r_1 r_2 \\cdots r_n = 33 \\]\nWe need to find the distinct integer roots whose product is 33. The possible sets of distinct integer factors of 33 are:\n\\[ \\{1, -1, 3, -3, 11, -11, 33, -33\\} \\]\nWe need to form a product of \\( n \\) distinct integers that equals 33. The possible factorizations are:\n\\[ 33 = 33 \\]\n\\[ 33 = 3 \\cdot 11 \\]\n\\[ 33 = (-33) \\]\n\\[ 33 = (-11) \\cdot (-3) \\]\n\\[ 33 = (-33) \\cdot 1 \\]\n\\[ 33 = 33 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 \\]\nThe only sets of distinct integers that work are \\( \\{1, 3, 11\\} \\) and \\( \\{-1, -3, -11\\} \\). We will use the set \\( \\{1, 3, 11\\} \\) for simplicity (the other set will give the same result for the sum of the roots taken one at a time).\n\n","prefix_end":1117,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":true,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[22043,279,47311,17767,282,2075,8,284,220,17,87,86167,488,264,15159,77,12,16,92,87,47822,77,12,16,92,488,1124,4385,2412,488,264,62,17,87,61,17,488,264,62,16,87,488,220,21,21,1124,8,448,7546,36829,323,12460,7546,19703,11,582,646,3158,279,47311,304,1181,2097,3018,1352,438,510,78045,282,2075,8,284,220,17,2075,481,435,62,16,2376,87,481,435,62,17,8,1124,4385,2412,320,87,481,435,1089,8,1124,921,2870,17767,435,62,16,11,435,62,17,11,1124,507,2412,11,435,1089,1124,8,525,279,12460,7546,19703,13,576,6783,4647,17767,264,62,15,1124,8